From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 23:50:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] onlineid: stand aside when this broker holds no token to
 answer with.

The project's own notes already say that claiming a provider one cannot serve
is worse than claiming none -- once a caller has committed to a broker, every
later failure reaches the user as a broker error instead of falling back to the
application's own sign-in. This broker was claiming one unconditionally.

Measured on a prefix whose token store had been emptied (the tokens expire in
hours; /tmp is aged out after ten days, so an empty store is the ordinary
state): Office asked for an account provider thirty-eight times in one
start-up, was handed one every time, then asked for six tokens and was told
there were none -- and never loaded WebView2 at all. The sign-in it can
actually complete never ran.

The store now decides. Tokens minted into it mean this broker has something to
answer with and takes the request; no tokens mean it stands aside, and the
application signs in through its own window, whose result is written into the
profile and survives a restart rather than living in a file that expires within
hours. All six provider lookups already route through create_provider_operation,
so one guard covers them.

After: thirteen "standing aside", no silent-token or account-enumeration calls
at all, and OneAuthWebView2Browser on screen ten seconds into start-up, twice
in a row.

(cherry picked from commit 5696c177c1663d5947920f0042c7830ec33cb01a)

diff --git a/dlls/windows.security.authentication.onlineid/webauth.c b/dlls/windows.security.authentication.onlineid/webauth.c
index cc39f0f252f..915701fae9b 100644
--- a/dlls/windows.security.authentication.onlineid/webauth.c
+++ b/dlls/windows.security.authentication.onlineid/webauth.c
@@ -519,6 +519,23 @@ static BOOL provider_is_supported(HSTRING id, HSTRING authority)
     return TRUE;
 }
 
+/* Whether this broker holds anything it could answer a token request with.
+ * The store's shape is described on load_authorized_token above. */
+static BOOL have_any_token(void)
+{
+    static const WCHAR pattern[] = L"Z:\\tmp\\office-wam-tokens\\*.txt";
+    static const WCHAR legacy[] = L"Z:\\tmp\\office-wam-token.txt";
+    WIN32_FIND_DATAW data;
+    HANDLE find;
+
+    if ((find = FindFirstFileW(pattern, &data)) != INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)
+    {
+        FindClose(find);
+        return TRUE;
+    }
+    return GetFileAttributesW(legacy) != INVALID_FILE_ATTRIBUTES;
+}
+
 static HRESULT create_provider_operation(HSTRING id, HSTRING authority, void **result)
 {
     struct provider *provider;
@@ -526,6 +543,25 @@ static HRESULT create_provider_operation(HSTRING id, HSTRING authority, void **r
     if (!result) return E_POINTER;
     *result = NULL;
 
+    /* Claiming a provider this broker cannot serve is worse than claiming
+     * none: once a caller has committed to a broker, every later failure
+     * reaches the user as a broker error instead of falling back to the
+     * application's own sign-in window.  Measured on an empty store: Office
+     * asked for a provider thirty-eight times in one start-up, was handed one
+     * every time, and never loaded WebView2 at all -- the sign-in it can
+     * actually complete never ran.
+     *
+     * So the store decides.  Tokens minted into it mean this broker has
+     * something to answer with and takes the request; no tokens mean it stands
+     * aside, and the application signs in through its own window -- which is
+     * also the path whose result is written into the profile and survives a
+     * restart, rather than living in a file that expires within hours. */
+    if (!have_any_token())
+    {
+        TRACE("no tokens held; standing aside so the caller uses its own sign-in.\n");
+        return create_object_operation(NULL, result);
+    }
+
     if (!provider_is_supported(id, authority))
     {
         TRACE("no account provider here for id %s authority %s.\n",
